<DiseasePage
  title="FSGS"
  description="FSGS (Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis) is the rare kidney disease at the center of our founder's own story."
  fullName="Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis"
  whatItIs="FSGS causes scarring in the glomeruli — the tiny filtering units inside the kidneys. As scar tissue builds up, the kidneys lose their ability to filter waste and excess fluid from the blood, which can progress to kidney failure requiring dialysis or a transplant. It can also recur after a transplant, attacking a new kidney the way it did the original ones."
  whoItAffects="FSGS can affect anyone, but it disproportionately affects Black Americans, partly linked to genetic risk factors like APOL1 gene variants. It is often diagnosed later in underserved communities, where access to nephrology care and early screening is limited."
  whyItMatters="FSGS is the disease our founder, Robert Sanchez, has lived with since 2009 — through one transplant, its recurrence, dialysis, and a second transplant in 2021. It's the reason The Jentosy Project exists."
/>
